+\section{Discussion and limitations}\label{sec:discussion}
+A certified bank preserves the LP rejection decision at integer targets while allowing its fractional value to differ. This permits a constant-coefficient circuit that avoids dynamically finding a fractional split. Coverage certificates are required because a merely valid Lagrangian bound can prune less than the LP. The root-tail construction is a safe baseline; smaller banks require additional coverage reasoning.
+
+These results support a compiler for the stated prefix predicate and quantify its reversible resource tradeoff. Lagrangian multiplier sets and integer rounding already occur in prior work \cite{bozejko2026}, and fractional-greedy bounds already occur in quantum knapsack B\&B comparisons \cite{wilkening2025}. The contribution concerns the all-prefix coverage construction and its implementation; minimum bank cardinality does not imply minimum gate count.
+
+The circuit contract requires positive integer weights, nonnegative integer profits, a fixed efficiency-ordered prefix, representable integer thresholds, and clean initial workspace. It returns no numerical LP value, integrality flag, completion witness, or LP-selected branching variable. Those outputs would require additional computation. Arithmetic coverage and gate-level simulation establish different properties; the initialization discrepancy demonstrates the need for preparation-only and independent dense-reference checks.
+
+The resource comparison assumes ideal arbitrary-angle gates and all-to-all connectivity. Hardware costs would additionally depend on routing, rotation synthesis, and error management. A complete search implementation would still need coherent node handling, branching and marking operations, reflections, error allocation, witness recovery, and preprocessing. Quantum-query frameworks \cite{ref35,ref24} provide context, but do not supply the missing implementation costs. The reported oracle resource reductions therefore do not establish an end-to-end runtime advantage.
+
+The resource tradeoff depends on the arithmetic layout and compilation model. Alternative implementations may change the relative gate counts, qubit requirements, and depth.
+
+\section{Conclusion}
+A certified static multiplier bank can preserve integer-threshold LP-pruning decisions for fixed efficiency-ordered knapsack prefixes. Coverage reduces to interval constraints, and the predicate can be expressed through bounded constant-coefficient arithmetic with reversible cleanup. Exact arithmetic checks cover the original and reduced-core instances, and separate small-circuit simulations test the reversible predicate. The matched root-aware comparison finds lower CX counts and qubit requirements throughout its 105-input cohort, together with increased depth on most inputs. These findings support a specific oracle construction and an explicit resource tradeoff; they do not establish physical or end-to-end quantum advantage.
+
